    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "Mathematics has always intrigued me. I used to enjoy it as a kid, especially when it came to simple problems. I didn’t have a lot of formal training, but I picked up ideas from my dad, who was a PhD in physics. I think it’s about patterns and organization. It helps me understand the world better, even if I don’t use it every day.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Yes, sometimes. When something is complex or unfamiliar, a little stress creeps in. That’s normal, I suppose. I used to be afraid of equations when I was younger. But now I just break them down into pieces. It helps to be patient. I don’t need to know everything right away.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Yes, I used a simple calculator app that explained each step. It helped me with basic algebra and economy problems. I like how it shows what to do next. It’s not perfect, but it’s useful. I didn’t use it for complicated topics. It’s a good tool for checking work.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "First, write down the equation. Then, get all terms to one side. Use the quadratic formula: x equals negative b plus or minus square root of b squared minus four a c, all over two a. Just plug in the numbers. The square root part might be tricky, so make sure you calculate it correctly. If it's negative, you'll get imaginary solutions.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "Differentiate the function. Set the derivative equal to zero. Solve for x. Plug each solution back into the original function to get y values. Those are the stationary points. If the derivative changes sign, it’s a turning point. If it stays the same, it’s a flat point.",
    "Briefly, how do you perform a Principal Component Analysis? Should I get anxious about its mathematics? Please, teach me.": "PCA reduces data to fewer components. First, standardize the data. Then, compute the covariance matrix. Find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors. Pick the components with the largest eigenvalues. Project the original data onto these new axes. The math is complex, but it’s useful for seeing patterns. Don’t get anxious—just follow the steps.",
    "According to you, how can LLMs be used to innovate math learning in schools and universities?": "LLMs can explain concepts in plain language. They can offer personalized feedback. Students can ask questions anytime. They can help fill in gaps for those who need extra support. It’s like having a tutor always available. But teachers still need to guide the learning."
